/*
Problem: Minimum Depth of Binary Tree (LC 111)
Topic: Binary Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

The minimum depth is the number of
nodes along the shortest path from
the root to a leaf.

Unlike maximum depth, we cannot
simply take:

min(left, right)

because one subtree may be empty.

If one child is missing, the path
must continue through the other
child.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. If the tree is empty,
   return 0.

2. Recursively compute the minimum
   depth of the left and right
   subtrees.

3. If the left child is missing,
   return right + 1.

4. If the right child is missing,
   return left + 1.

5. Otherwise, return:

   min(left, right) + 1

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

      3
     / \
    9   20
       /  \
      15   7

Minimum depth:

2

Path:

3 → 9

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

    2
     \
      3
       \
        4

Minimum depth:

3

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

A missing child cannot contribute to
a valid root-to-leaf path.

Only existing paths are considered
when computing the minimum depth.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Every node is visited once.

Space : O(h)

h = Height of the tree due to the
recursion stack.

Worst case: O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Tree / Recursion
*/

class Solution {
public:

    int minDepth(TreeNode* root) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return 0;

        int left = minDepth(root->left);
        int right = minDepth(root->right);

        // Only right subtree exists
        if (root->left == NULL)
            return right + 1;

        // Only left subtree exists
        if (root->right == NULL)
            return left + 1;

        // Both subtrees exist
        return min(left, right) + 1;
    }
};
